The prior account treats the flatmate's withdrawal from chore conversations as a datum to be worked around, not a message to be read. I want to pose the problem differently: not 'what procedure should we adopt?' but 'what is each person's actual position, and how would we find out without another conversation?'

Consider that the avoiding flatmate may not be avoiding chores at all. They may be avoiding a specific recurring interaction — one in which positions harden, nothing is recorded, and the same ground is re-covered. That is not the same as refusing to contribute. If it is the pattern rather than the person that is being avoided, then the prior account's written list is aimed at the right target for the wrong reason: it helps not because it makes agreements explicit but because it replaces an interaction that has become aversive with an artifact that can be edited privately.

But I should be careful. 'They are avoiding the pattern, not the chores' is a hypothesis, and the material does not settle it. What the material does support is weaker: the three of us have no shared record of what was agreed, so every re-encounter reopens the same dispute. A written list would create such a record. Whether the avoidance then diminishes is an empirical question, not something I can reason my way to.

A second reframing: the problem may not be the chores at all. Three people sharing a flat, with changing schedules and no standing review, will accumulate unresolved friction that surfaces wherever attention lands. Chores are simply the most legible site. If so, no chore procedure will hold, because the underlying load — who notices what, who carries the mental list, who feels responsible for the tone of the household — remains unaddressed. The prior account gestures at this ('symptoms rather than causes') but then proceeds as if chores were the unit of work.

I do not have enough to choose between these reframings. What I can say is that the prior account's first move — draft a short list, circulate it, invite written objection — is compatible with all three, which is a point in its favor and also a limit: compatibility with every diagnosis is not evidence for any of them.

What I would add to it is a check before the draft: ask each person separately, not in a group conversation, what they think has been going wrong. The point is not to reach consensus but to see whether the three accounts overlap. If they do, the draft can be short. If they do not, the draft is premature and the disagreement is at a different level than the list can reach.

The cost is real: three separate conversations, each of which may feel like a grievance session. I would still take it, because a draft built on a misread of what anyone wants will be discarded quietly, and then there will be less appetite for a second attempt.